  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper text is present. Some figure and table text is flattened or garbled, but the principal result tables and prose are readable.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study tests whether changing a network's sharpness while approximately preserving training loss changes generalization, using optimum shifting and Hessian trace rather than the versions' specified tests.",
    "gate_note": "The paper applies stochastic optimum shifting (SOS) to trained models and during training, and reports test accuracy, training-loss changes, and Hessian-trace results. These bear on the broad sharpness–generalization question, but do not measure magnitude-scaled perturbation tolerance or compare its ranking with norm- and margin-based measures as V7 specifies. Rows are therefore mapped to none of the listed versions.",
    "test_reason": "The study uses Hessian-trace sharpness to motivate SOS and evaluates its performance. It does not measure V7's magnitude-scaled perturbation tolerance or its ranking of generalization gaps against other measures. Its remaining experiments test none of V1–V8.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The paraphrase captures the authors' conclusion: SOS preserves empirical loss, reduces Hessian trace and improves generalization across vision tasks. Their conclusion is stronger than the supplied evidence: early-stage loss rises slightly, and Table 5 reports detection mAP decreases despite prose claiming improvements.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study uses a constrained least-Frobenius-norm parameter shift in the final linear layer, including stochastic batch-based shifting; this differs from the listed original-source methods. It uses standard SGD and SAM as training comparisons.",
    "method_sensitivity": "SOS batch size affects performance: the authors report an initial rise and then decline in improvement as batch size increases, with batch size 300 best in their CIFAR-100 experiment. The paper also reports that early checkpoints show a slight training-loss increase, while later checkpoints retain loss nearly unchanged. Results vary by architecture, dataset, augmentation, and training scheme.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Tables 1–3 report higher classification accuracy after SOS or with SOS during training. These evaluate the proposed method rather than any listed claim version.",
        "proposed_claim": "SOS can improve held-out classification accuracy on the tested vision models and datasets.",
