    "peer_reviewed": "AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence",
    "problems": "The reported experiments concern image classification and detection; the paper says SOS depends on Neural Collapse and is currently limited to image tasks. Stochastic SOS only preserves loss on a small batch, with slight loss increases at early training stages. Several Hessian results are figure-only, and the extracted Figure 2 panel labels are partly inconsistent.",
    "rows_left_out": "Table 1 also reports ResNet-50 and DenseNet-201 across CIFAR-10/100 and augmentation conditions; Table 2 reports Shufflenet_V2 and ResNext50; Table 3 reports additional model, dataset, augmentation, and SGD/SAM comparisons; Table 4 varies SOS batch size; Table 5 reports YOLOv5s and YOLOv5x mAP; Appendix Table 6 compares weight-decay ablations. These separately reported outcomes were not extracted to keep the record within 60 rows and prioritize representative explicit comparisons. Figures 2, 4, and 7–10 contain additional Hessian-trace and weight trajectories, mostly figure-only.",
